<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Baghdad (1258)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1258">1258</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Mongol destruction of Baghdad, Mongol invasion of 1258, Mongol sack of Baghdad, sack of Baghdad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1258 was the capture and sack of the Abbasid capital by the Mongol army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim">al-Musta'sim</a> was executed and much of the city's population killed. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> treat the event as the end of the caliphate in Baghdad, and accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> note its impact on the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-baghdad-1258.jpg" alt="Siege of Baghdad (1258)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sayf al-vâhidî et al. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bagdad1258.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Even after the <b>Mongol destruction of Baghdad</b> in 1258, the shadow caliphate maintained by the Mamluks in Cairo (1261–1517) served as an important legitimising institution for Muslim rulers across North Africa and the Levant.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruler was at first an emir, but Abu Zakariya’s son Muhammad I al-Mustansir (r. 1249–1277) took the caliphal title of commander of the faithful in 1253, and after the <b>Mongol sack of Baghdad</b> in 1258 he was briefly recognised as caliph by the Sharif of Mecca.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Mongol sack of Baghdad</b> in 1258, in which the last caliph of Baghdad was put to death, ended that revival.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li></ul></section>
